From a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Sat, 15 Mar 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] SurfaceFlinger: Skip refresh rate reselection during
 initial display bring-up

On GSI targets with multi-rate displays (e.g. 60Hz+120Hz),
onNewFrontInternalDisplay calls applyRefreshRateSelectorPolicy during
init. With no content layers, getRankedFrameRates uses Descending
order, selecting the highest refresh rate (120Hz). The resulting
setDesiredMode defers the mode switch to the first presentDisplay
commit, which can cause vendor HWC implementations to destroy the
composer client or fail the atomic commit, killing SurfaceFlinger.

Skip applyRefreshRateSelectorPolicy on the initial display bring-up
(oldFrontInternalDisplayPtr == nullptr), preserving the HWC boot
default (typically 60Hz). DisplayManager will set the appropriate
policy once the system is fully running. Subsequent display changes
(hotplug, foldable switches) still re-evaluate as before.
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 11 ++++++++---
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index 549d2a4..f3a9b1c 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -8986,9 +8986,14 @@ void SurfaceFlinger::onNewFrontInternalDisplay(const DisplayDevice* oldFrontInte
 
         mScheduler->designatePacesetterDisplay(mFrontInternalDisplayId);
 
-        // Whether or not the policy of the new front internal display changed while it was powered
-        // off (in which case its preferred mode has already been propagated to HWC via
-        // setDesiredMode), the Scheduler's emittedModeOpt must be initialized to the newly
+        // [GSI] During initial display bring-up (no previous display), skip
+        // applyRefreshRateSelectorPolicy to avoid re-ranking modes with no
+        // layers, which selects the highest refresh rate and triggers a mode
+        // change that can crash vendor HWC implementations. DisplayManager
+        // will apply the appropriate policy once the system is fully running.
+        if (!oldFrontInternalDisplayPtr) return;
+
+        // For subsequent display changes (hotplug, fold/unfold), the Scheduler's emittedModeOpt must be initialized to the newly
         // active mode, and the kernel idle timer of the front internal display must be toggled.
         applyRefreshRateSelectorPolicy(mFrontInternalDisplayId,
                                        newFrontInternalDisplay.refreshRateSelector());
